WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.480
Today we are going to see how NVIDIA’s incredible
light transport algorithm can render these

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:18.520
notoriously difficult photorealistic smoke
plumes, volumetric bunnies, and even explosions

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.180
interactively.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.940
We are going to talk about this amazing paper
in a moment, and given that NVIDIA can already

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:33.260
render this absolutely beautiful marbles demo
in real time, not only with real time light

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:39.960
transport, but also with our other favorite
around here, real time physics!

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.670
This is just to say that tech transfer is
happening here, the papers are real, the papers

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:52.020
that you see here really make it to real applications
that everyone can use at home.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.770
Please note that they won’t say exactly
what tech is under the hood here, but looking

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.559
at their best light transport papers might
be a good starting point of what is to come

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:03.780
in the future.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.639
Now, back to the intro.

00:01:05.639 --> 00:01:10.319
What you are seeing here should not be possible
at all!

00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:11.319
Why is that?

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:17.310
Well, when we use a light transport simulation
algorithm to create such an image, we get

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.209
a photorealistic image, but as you see, not
immediately.

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:23.209
Not even close.

00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:29.119
It is a miracle of science that with this,
we can shoot millions and millions of light

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:35.880
rays into the scene to estimate how much light
is bouncing around, and initially, the inaccuracies

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.430
in our estimations show up as noise in these
images.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:45.200
As we shoot more and more rays, this clears
up over time.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.619
However, there are two problems.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:52.219
One, this can take hours to finish.

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:59.109
And in the case of volumetric light transport,
these light rays can bounce, scatter, and

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:06.250
get absorbed inside a dense medium, in that
case, it takes much, much longer.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.039
Not only hours, sometimes, even days.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:11.330
Oh my goodness.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.480
So, how did NVIDIA pull this off?

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:21.909
Well, they built on a previous technique of
theirs that is capable of rendering 3.4 million

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:29.110
light sources, and not just for one image,
but it can even handle animation.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.750
Just look at all this beautiful footage.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:37.709
Now it would be so great to have a variant
of this that works on volumetric effects,

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:43.750
such as explosions and smoke plumes, but of
course, anyone who has tried it before says

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.969
that there is no chance that those could run
interactively.

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:49.569
No chance at all.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:53.280
Well, scientists at NVIDIA beg to differ.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.280
Check this out.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.959
This is a previous technique and it says 12
spp.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:05.420
That means 12 samples per pixel, which is
more or less simulating 12 light rays for

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.940
each pixel of these images.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.879
That is not a lot of information, so let’s
have a closer look at what this previous method

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:14.359
can do with that.

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:21.500
Well, there is still tons of noise, and it
flickers a lot when we rotate the camera around.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:29.049
Well, I would say there is not much hope here,
we would need hundreds, maybe even thousands

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:33.640
of samples per pixel to get a clean image,
not 12.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.939
And, look, it gets even worse.

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:37.939
What?

00:03:37.939 --> 00:03:45.840
The new technique runs not thousands, not
hundreds, and not even 12 samples per pixel

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.409
in the same amount of time, but…really?

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:50.780
Can this be?

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.159
1 sample per pixel?

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:55.900
Can you do anything meaningful with that?

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.250
Well, hold on to your papers, and check this
out.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.209
Wow, look at that!

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:08.590
It can do much better with just one ray per
pixel than the previous technique can do with

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.060
12.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.430
That is absolutely incredible.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.609
I really don’t know what to say.

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:19.549
And it works not just on this example, but
on many others as well.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:25.220
This is so far ahead of the previous technique
that it seems like science fiction.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.350
I absolutely love it.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:34.830
However, yes I can hear you asking, Károly,
but these are still noisy, so why get so excited

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.110
for all these incomplete results?

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:43.270
Well, do you remember what we did with the
previous NVIDIA light transport paper?

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:48.860
We took these noisy inputs, and plugged them
into a denoising technique that is specifically

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.350
designed for light transport algorithms.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.530
It tries to guess what is behind the noise.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.400
And as you see, these can help a ton.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:08.060
But you know, can they help so much that a
still noisy input, a meager 1 sample per pixel

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.349
can become usable?

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:11.889
Well, let’s have a look.

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:14.110
Oh my goodness.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.290
Look at that.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:21.280
The result is clearly not perfect, one light
ray per pixel can hardly give us a perfect

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.710
image, but after denoising, this is unreal.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.360
We are almost there right away!

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:34.520
It has so much less flickering than the previous
technique with much more samples, and we are

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.980
experienced Fellow Scholars around here, so
let’s also check the amount of detail in

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.200
the image.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.240
And…whoa!

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.570
There is no contest here.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:52.720
This technique also pulls off all this wizardry
by trying to reuse information that is otherwise

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.370
typically thrown away.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:06:01.199
For instance, with no reuse, we get this baseline
result, and if we reuse information from a

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:05.490
previous frame in the animation, we get this.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.780
That is significantly better than the baseline.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.250
If we reuse previous rays spatially, that
is also an improvement.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.310
So are these different kinds of improvements?

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:25.350
Well, let’s add them together, and oh yes,
now this is what we are here for.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.940
Look at how much more information there is
in this image.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:34.020
So now, even these amazing volumetric scenes
can be rendered interactively?

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.240
I am out of words.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.730
What a time to be alive!

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:45.210
And, if you feel inspired by these results,
I have a free Master-level course on light

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:51.120
transport where we write a full light simulation
program from scratch, and learn about physics,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.629
the world around us, and more.

00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:57.060
If you watch it, you will see the world differently.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.650
That is free education for everyone, that’s
what I want.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:23.759
So, the course is available free of charge
for everyone, no strings attached, check it

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:47.539
out 

00:07:47.539 --> 00:08:07.069
in the video description.

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:09.279
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

